[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now delivery — and one constraint I want to state plainly rather than bury in the spec, because it limits what this
  can honestly promise:

  [1mA browser-only app can only notify you while the page is open.[0m There's no reliable way for a static page to wake
  itself and fire an alert at 9am when the tab is closed. (Service workers can't schedule timers reliably; the
  Notification Triggers API never shipped broadly; real scheduled push needs a server.) So the practical behavior is:
  alerts fire live while the tab is open, and anything that came due while it was closed gets shown as a catch-up batch
  on next load. If waking you when the app isn't running is essential, that's the server answer and we should revisit
  the earlier fork.

  Given that:

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should a notification actually reach you? → In-app notification list, Toast on fire[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before the design.

  On stack, I'm going to recommend [1mvanilla ES modules, no build step[0m unless you object — the app is a list and a timer;
  React plus a bundler would be more infrastructure than feature. Say the word if you'd rather have a framework.

  Tooling is cheapest to set up now, before any code exists:

